# env.example — names only, never values.
#
# Copy this to .env.local, put the real values there, and never commit that file.
# This file is committed, so the next person can see what the app expects.
#
# The Monday GTM Dashboard needs nothing here. It is one HTML file with the CRM data
# inside it: no API, no key, no server. The file exists to show the form, and so the
# first credential this app ever needs has an obvious place to go.
#
# Add a name here only when the code actually reads it. A name listed here that
# nothing reads is a name the next person spends an afternoon hunting a value for.
#
# The one thing that can be pointed somewhere else is the CSV, and it is a query
# parameter rather than an environment variable: open the file with ?csv=<url> and
# it fetches that instead. Nothing in the app reads the environment.


# --- What this file would hold once the app has a backend ---
#
# Names only. Real values live in .env.local or in the host's secret manager.
# Anything marked server-only must never reach browser code, because a browser
# cannot keep a secret.

# SUPABASE_URL=
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=    # server only
# CRM_API_TOKEN=                # server only
# SESSION_SECRET=               # server only
